    "raw": "{\"ruleId\":\"item-31d4f6377408\",\"class\":\"partial\",\"constraints\":[{\"id\":\"nip22-kind1111-inline-html-content\",\"type\":\"forbiddenPattern\",\"pattern\":\"\\\\bkind[\\\"']?\\\\s*[:=]\\\\s*1111\\\\b.*\\\\bcontent[\\\"']?\\\\s*[:=]\\\\s*[\\\"'`][^\\\"'`]*<\\\\/?[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9]*[\\\\s>\\\\/]|\\\\bcontent[\\\"']?\\\\s*[:=]\\\\s*[\\\"'`][^\\\"'`]*<\\\\/?[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9]*[\\\\s>\\\\/].*\\\\bkind[\\\"']?\\\\s*[:=]\\\\s*1111\\\\b\",\"path\":\".floor/added/**\"}],\"coverage\":\"Only the flagrant case where a single added line builds an event literal with kind 1111 and a string-literal content holding an HTML tag. Keys may be written in either order, quoted or bare, with ':' or '='.\",\"residual\":\"Does the hunk compose kind 1111 comment content that contains HTML, Markdown or other formatting, or record an inline quoted event as an `e` tag instead of a `q` tag, in a form other than a single line pairing kind 1111 with a literal HTML-tagged content string?\",\"probes\":{\"violating\":[{\"patch\":\"--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/src/nostr/comment.ts\\n@@ -0,0 +1 @@\\n+export const ev = { kind: 1111, content: \\\"<b>hi</b>\\\", tags: [] };\\n\"},{\"patch\":\"--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/nostr/comment.py\\n@@ -0,0 +1 @@\\n+ev = {\\\"content\\\": \\\"<p>hello</p>\\\", \\\"kind\\\": 1111}\\n\"}],\"compliant\":{\"patch\":\"--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/src/nostr/comment.ts\\n@@ -0,0 +1 @@\\n+export const ev = { kind: 1111, content: \\\"hello world\\\", tags: [[\\\"q\\\", quotedId]] };\\n\"}},\"rationale\":\"Deciding whether content is built for kind 1111 across several lines, and whether it carries Markdown or other formatting, needs data flow and meaning. So does telling an inline quote recorded as an `e` tag from a legitimate reply `e` tag. Only a single line pairing kind 1111 with literal HTML content is mechanical. The file-extension scope cannot be expressed with one glob, so non-code added files are also scanned.\"}",
